Fillets: a ball rolling on an edge before a blend curving away (patch
1230) turns over a sliver past the seam of the face its other contact lies
on, a face closed round its periodic surface (mitcad#1370).

The case is patch 1230's: a ring's top, its inner rim rounded with 2.5,
the ball rolling on the top's far edges and passing over sliver edges of a
few micrometres where fins' crests meet the top. Since faces on one
surface are joined after each feature, the body before the rounding has
its inner cylinder as one face closed round its axis, its seam under a
crest at one of the mouths, and the rim as one closed edge. There the
ball reaches the end of the far edge it rolled on with its contact on the
cylinder 0.15 micrometres short of the seam, and its turn about that end
over the 6.5-micrometre sliver carries that contact across the seam. The
surface/edge walk found the contact outside its face's parameters past
the seam, refused each step, and the rounding failed. Before, the
cylinder was cut at that mouth into faces of their own, and patch 1230
moved the contact onto the next one at the edge between them.

1. PerformSetOfSurfOnElSpine: patch 1230's change of face for the other
   contact, within a hair of an edge of its face ahead at the end of the
   edge rolled on, takes the seam of a face closed round its periodic
   surface too (an edge used both ways in the face; two faces on one
   surface share an edge with two curves on it as well, each using it
   once). The contact goes on in the same face on the seam's other curve,
   where the point a little ahead lies nearer that curve than the contact
   does; the piece before ends on the seam, its use on the side it leaves,
   with the walk's direction as its vector (ChangeTransition takes the next
   piece's start by it). The walk starts there as on its face, with
   nothing to find on the face's boundary.
2. BRepBlend_SurfRstLineBuilder::Perform: a turn about the restriction's
   end that an earlier Perform of the same walk found (StartPivot), as
   ChFi3d_Builder::ComputeData walks again with a smaller step where the
   first walk gave too few points, is found again from the start, the
   section solved with the ball pinned there. Kept, the walk began from
   the start's free section, tens of nanometres off the turn's, and each of
   its first steps was refused as too large. A turn found by
   PerformFirstSection is kept as before.
Every other fillet is as before.

On item 36's bodies of two versions of the design (the replay's body after
the rounding of the outer rim, its faces on one surface joined): valid,
244 and 261 faces; the bodies of patch 1230's test as before.

Upstream: new.

